Change files: release intent and release notes, one file per change
====================================================================

Every PR that changes a workspace crate adds a change file here. A
release (`just release`) builds its notes from the change files ADDED
since the previous tag, read at the tagged commit, and puts them in the
GitHub Release. Nothing is deleted or committed: the files stay here as
the record. CHANGELOG.md ends at 26.10.1 and is no longer written. CI
enforces the files on every PR (`cargo rail change check`; locally:
`just ci-changes`). To fix a note, edit its file on main before releasing.

Write one with cargo-rail (in the dev or CI shell):

    cargo rail change add <every crate you changed> --bump <none|patch|minor|major> \
      -m "Fixed sync stalling after offline edits"

It writes .changes/<slug>.md:

    ---
    "files-store" = "patch"
    ---

    Fixed sync stalling after offline edits

- The message is the release note, written for the user: start with a
  verb (Added / Fixed / Improved / Changed / Removed / Deprecated /
  Security); name any changed setting, command or keybinding exactly.
- --bump: major -> "Breaking / Migration", minor -> "Added",
  patch -> "Fixed and improved". none = nothing a user would notice
  (refactor, CI, tests, docs): it satisfies the check and adds no entry.
- Breaking changes (a PR title with `!`) and anything under
  **/migrations/**: --bump major, and add a "Migration:" paragraph to the
  body (what changed, why, exact steps, whether a DB migration runs on
  deploy).
- A revert of something not yet released: delete the original change file
  instead of adding a new one.

The bump is release intent and severity only: the app version is CalVer,
computed at release time.

This file has no .md extension on purpose: cargo-rail parses every .md in
this directory as a change file.

Task's release flow: skills/release-workflow.md (tag-from-main). The
fleet `release-workflow` skill holds the shared change-file rules
(fleet-skills, skills/release-workflow/references/changelog.md and
cargo-rail.md).
